(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-eye-ear-anatomy-quiz"]={"title":"Eye and Ear Anatomy — Anatomy Exam 2","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What are the medial and lateral canthus, and the palpebral fissure?","choices":["Canthus connects the eyelids at the corners of the eye; palpebral fissure is the area between the eyelids","Canthus is the area between the eyelids; palpebral fissure connects the eyelids at the corners of the eye","Neither structure is related to the eyelids; both instead describe the boundaries of the bony orbit","Both terms describe the same structure, referring collectively to the entire eyelid margin"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial and lateral canthus connect the eyelids at the corners of the eye, while the palpebral fissure is the area between the eyelids. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What do eyelashes contain, and what reflex do they trigger?","choices":["Contain lacrimal gland tissue, triggering pupil constriction","Contain sebaceous glands only, with no nerve association","Contain the root hair plexus, which triggers the blinking reflex","Contain the conjunctiva, triggering tear production"],"answer":2,"exp":"Eyelashes contain the root hair plexus, which triggers the blinking reflex. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the conjunctiva, and what are its two subdivisions?","choices":["Epithelial lining of the eyelids/eye surface; subdivided into palpebral conjunctiva (inner eyelid lining) and bulbar/ocular conjunctiva (outer eye lining)","A gland producing tears; subdivided into lacrimal and tarsal glands lining the upper and lower eyelids","A muscle moving the eye; subdivided into the rectus and oblique groups controlling gaze direction","The colored part of the eye that regulates pupil size; subdivided into the iris proper and the sphincter and dilator pupillae muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The conjunctiva is the epithelial lining of the eyelids and outer eye surface, with fluid production helping prevent it from drying out; it is subdivided into the palpebral conjunctiva (inner lining of the eyelids) and the bulbar (ocular) conjunctiva (outer lining of the eye). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What do tarsal glands secrete, and what happens if a tarsal gland becomes infected?","choices":["Secrete mucus for the nasal cavity; infection causes chronic sinus congestion","Secrete tears; infection may result in excessive, uncontrolled tearing","Secrete a lipid-rich product keeping eyelids from sticking together; infection may result in a cyst","Secrete aqueous humor; infection has no clinical significance for the eyelid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Tarsal glands secrete a lipid-rich product that keeps the eyelids from sticking together (located along the inner margin of the eyelids); an infection of the tarsal gland may result in a cyst. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What structures make up the lacrimal apparatus, and what is its overall pathway?","choices":["Only the lacrimal gland, with no duct system, secreting tears directly onto the corneal surface without drainage","The cornea, iris, and pupil, which work together to focus incoming light and regulate the amount reaching the retina through the pupil's aperture","Lacrimal glands (produce tears), lacrimal canaliculi, lacrimal sac, and nasolacrimal duct; tears flow over the ocular surface then into the nasal cavity via the nasolacrimal canal","Tarsal glands, glands of Zeis, and the conjunctiva, which jointly produce and distribute the lipid layer of tears"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lacrimal apparatus consists of the lacrimal glands (which produce tears), lacrimal canaliculi, lacrimal sac, and nasolacrimal duct (canal); tears flow over the ocular surface and into the nasolacrimal canal, which enters the nasal cavity — explaining why heavy crying causes a \"runny\" nose. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates the lateral rectus muscle, and which innervates the superior oblique?","choices":["Neither muscle is innervated by a cranial nerve","Lateral rectus: CN VI; Superior oblique: CN IV","Both are innervated by CN III","Lateral rectus: CN IV; Superior oblique: CN VI"],"answer":1,"exp":"Per the \"LR6, SO4, all the rest are 3\" mnemonic, the lateral rectus is innervated by CN VI, and the superior oblique is innervated by CN IV. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique muscles?","choices":["CN III","CN VII","CN VI","CN IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"CN III innervates the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique muscles (\"all the rest are 3\"). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What movement does the superior oblique (SO) produce, and what movement does the inferior oblique (IO) produce?","choices":["SO: up and out; IO: down and out","SO: in; IO: out","SO: down and out; IO: up and out","Both muscles produce identical movement"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior oblique (SO) produces movement down and out, while the inferior oblique (IO) produces movement up and out. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What movement does the medial rectus (MR) produce, and what movement does the lateral rectus (LR) produce?","choices":["MR: out; LR: in","MR: up; LR: down","Both muscles produce the same movement","MR: in; LR: out"],"answer":3,"exp":"The medial rectus (MR) moves the eye in (medially), while the lateral rectus (LR) moves the eye out (laterally). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are the three tunics of the eye, and what does each correspond to?","choices":["Fibrous tunic (sclera/cornea), vascular tunic (iris/ciliary body/choroid), and neural tunic (retina)","Fibrous, vascular, and neural tunics all describe different regions of the sclera alone","Only two tunics exist: fibrous and neural, with vascular supply distributed diffusely throughout both","Fibrous tunic (choroid), vascular tunic (retina), and neural tunic (sclera)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three tunics of the eye are the fibrous tunic (making up the sclera and cornea), the vascular tunic (including the iris, ciliary body, and choroid), and the neural tunic (also called the retina). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the fibrous tunic (outer layer) of the eye make up, and what is its function?","choices":["Makes up the iris and ciliary body; regulates the amount of light entering the pupil","Makes up the sclera and cornea; provides protection and attachment sites for extra-ocular muscles","Makes up the retina; converts light into neural signals sent along the optic nerve","Makes up the lens only; focuses incoming light directly onto the retina's surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fibrous tunic (outer layer) makes up the sclera and cornea, providing some degree of protection and attachment sites for the extra-ocular muscles; the cornea is described as modified sclera. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does the vascular tunic (middle layer) of the eye consist of, and what are its functions?","choices":["Consists only of rods and cones; converts light to neural signals sent along the optic nerve to the visual cortex","Consists of the lens only; focuses light onto the retina by changing curvature during accommodation","Consists of blood vessels, lymphatics, and intrinsic eye muscles; regulates light entering the eye, secretes/reabsorbs aqueous fluid, and controls lens shape","Consists only of the sclera and cornea; provides protection only, with no role in fluid regulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vascular tunic (middle layer) consists of blood vessels, lymphatics, and intrinsic eye muscles; it regulates the amount of light entering the eye, secretes and reabsorbs aqueous fluid, and controls the shape of the lens, including the iris, ciliary body, and choroid. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does the iris consist of, and what determines eye color?","choices":["Consists of the lens and ciliary body only, with color determined by the density of the lens fibers","Consists of rods and cones, which create eye color through differing photopigment concentrations","Consists only of smooth muscle, with no pigment; eye color instead comes from the underlying choroid","Consists of blood vessels, pigment, and smooth muscles; the pigment creates eye color, and smooth muscles change pupil diameter"],"answer":3,"exp":"The iris consists of blood vessels, pigment, and smooth muscles; the pigment creates the color of the eye, and the smooth muscles contract to change the diameter of the pupil. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does the ciliary body consist of, and what is it connected to?","choices":["The pigment layer of the retina, connected directly to the optic nerve sheath","The sclera and cornea directly, connected by a ring of collagen fibers","Only blood vessels, with no muscular component, connected to the choroid","Ciliary muscles connected to suspensory ligaments, which are connected to the lens"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ciliary body consists of ciliary muscles connected to suspensory ligaments, which are in turn connected to the lens. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the choroid, and what does its innermost portion attach to?","choices":["A highly vascularized structure whose innermost portion attaches to the outermost portion of the retina","The pigmented outer layer of the cornea, attaching to the sclera at the limbus","An avascular structure unrelated to the retina, attaching instead to the ciliary body","A muscle controlling pupil diameter, attaching to the iris at its outer margin"],"answer":0,"exp":"The choroid is highly vascularized, and its innermost portion attaches to the outermost portion of the retina. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the neural tunic also called, and what two layers does it consist of?","choices":["Also called the retina; consists of a pigmented layer (outer) and a neural layer (inner)","Also called the choroid; consists of only one layer, the pigmented epithelium","Also called the ciliary body; consists of muscle layers only, with no photoreceptors","Also called the sclera; consists of the cornea and lens working together"],"answer":0,"exp":"The neural tunic is also called the retina, made of two layers: the pigmented layer (outer layer) and the neural layer (inner layer). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the difference between rods and cones in the retina?","choices":["Both cell types serve identical functions","Neither cell type is a photoreceptor","Rods are for color vision; cones are for night vision","Rods are for night vision; cones are for color vision"],"answer":3,"exp":"The retina's cells consist of rods (for night vision) and cones (for color vision). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is unique about the fovea centralis, and what happens if an object is focused on the optic disc?","choices":["Both areas have an identical rod/cone ratio, since photoreceptor distribution is uniform across the entire retina","Fovea centralis is 100% rods; optic disc is where color vision is best due to its dense ganglion cell layer","Fovea centralis is 100% cones (best color vision); optic disc has 0% rods or cones, so vision does not occur there (the \"blind spot\")","The optic disc has the highest concentration of cones in the retina, giving it the sharpest visual acuity"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fovea centralis is 100% cones, giving the best color vision when an object is focused there, while the optic disc has 0% rods or cones — if an object is focused on this area, vision does not occur, hence it is known as the \"blind spot.\" — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Why can we see color during the day but not at night, per the material's explanation?","choices":["Color vision is unrelated to rod/cone activity, depending instead entirely on wavelength sensitivity of the pigment epithelium","Rods require light to be stimulated; cones activate at night once ambient light drops below a critical threshold","Both rods and cones are equally active regardless of light level, with color perception instead governed by pupil diameter","Cones require light to be stimulated (enabling color vision); at night, cones deactivate and rods activate instead (enabling night vision without color discrimination)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cones require light to be stimulated, which is why we see color during the day; at night (with at least a small amount of light), the cones deactivate and the rods begin to be activated, allowing night vision but not color determination. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are the anterior cavity and posterior cavity of the eye, and what fluids fill each?","choices":["Anterior cavity (anterior and posterior chambers) filled with aqueous fluid; posterior cavity (vitreous chamber) filled with vitreous fluid","Both cavities are filled with the same fluid, which circulates freely between the anterior and posterior segments","Neither cavity contains fluid; both are instead filled with a soft, collagenous connective tissue","Anterior chamber filled with vitreous gel; posterior chamber filled with watery aqueous fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"The anterior cavity (consisting of the anterior and posterior chambers) is filled with aqueous fluid, while the posterior cavity (vitreous chamber) is filled with vitreous fluid. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the pathway of aqueous fluid (humor), from secretion to entering venous circulation?","choices":["Secreted by cells at the ciliary body → posterior chamber → through the pupil → anterior chamber → canal of Schlemm → venous circulation","Secreted by the retina → vitreous chamber → canal of Schlemm → venous circulation, bypassing the anterior chamber","Secreted by the lens → posterior chamber only, with no drainage pathway, accumulating until reabsorbed by the vitreous","Secreted by the cornea → anterior chamber only, with no further flow, remaining static until reabsorbed locally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aqueous fluid (humor) is secreted by cells at the ciliary body, enters the posterior chamber (posterior of the iris), flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber, flows through the canal of Schlemm, and enters venous circulation. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are the functions of vitreous fluid (humor)?","choices":["Only regulates intraocular pressure via the canal of Schlemm, with no structural role","Only lubricates the outer surface of the eye, preventing corneal drying","Produces the pigment for eye color, released from the ciliary body","Supports the shape of the eye, the position of the lens, and the position of the retina"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vitreous fluid, a gelatinous material in the posterior chamber, supports the shape of the eye, the position of the lens, and the position of the retina. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the correct order light waves travel through the eye, and how is the signal transmitted to the brain?","choices":["Retina → lens → cornea → pupil → transmitted via CN VIII, the vestibulocochlear nerve","Pupil → lens → cornea → retina → transmitted via CN I, the olfactory nerve","Cornea → lens → pupil → retina → anterior chamber → transmitted via CN IV","Cornea → anterior chamber → pupil → posterior chamber → lens → focuses on retina → depolarization → transmitted via CN II"],"answer":3,"exp":"Light waves pass through the cornea, the anterior chamber, the pupil, the posterior chamber, and the lens, which focuses the image on the retina, creating depolarization of neural cells; the signal is then transmitted to the brain via CN II (the optic nerve). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which extrinsic eye muscle elevates the upper eyelid, and which cranial nerve controls it?","choices":["Inferior rectus muscle; CN III","Superior oblique muscle; CN IV","Lateral rectus; CN VI innervates it to elevate the eyelid","Levator palpebrae superioris; CN III"],"answer":3,"exp":"The levator palpebrae superioris elevates the upper eyelid and is controlled by CN III, alongside the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are the glands of Zeis, and what are they associated with?","choices":["Glands that secrete a lipid-rich product along the inner eyelid margin","Glands that produce thick secretions within the canthus","Glands that produce tears, located superiorly and laterally","Sebaceous glands associated with the eyelashes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glands of Zeis are sebaceous glands associated with the eyelashes. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What do lacrimal caruncle glands produce, and where are they located?","choices":["Sebum; associated with the eyelashes","Thick secretions; located within the canthus areas","Tears; located at the superior, lateral portion of the eye","A lipid-rich product; located along the inner eyelid margin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lacrimal caruncle glands produce thick secretions and are located within the canthus areas. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"An infection of which glands (other than the tarsal gland) may result in a sty?","choices":["Only the ciliary body, since it is the sole gland embedded within the eyelid margin","No gland infection produces a sty; only a tarsal gland infection produces a cyst","Glands of Zeis, lacrimal glands, or lacrimal caruncle glands (any gland other than the tarsal gland)","Only the tarsal gland, since it is the largest gland embedded within the eyelid"],"answer":2,"exp":"An infection of any of the eye's glands other than the tarsal gland (e.g., glands of Zeis, lacrimal glands, lacrimal caruncle glands) may result in a sty, whereas a tarsal gland infection specifically results in a cyst. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How many extrinsic muscles move the eye, and how many primary actions is each associated with?","choices":["4 extrinsic muscles, each associated with two primary actions","6 extrinsic muscles, each associated with one primary action","8 extrinsic muscles, each associated with no primary action","6 extrinsic muscles, all sharing the same single primary action"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are 6 extrinsic muscles that move the eye in many directions, and each muscle is associated with one primary action. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"The cornea is described as what, in relation to the sclera?","choices":["An entirely separate tissue type unrelated to the sclera","Part of the vascular tunic, not the fibrous tunic","A component of the neural tunic","Modified sclera"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cornea is modified sclera, both belonging to the fibrous tunic (outer layer) of the eye. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Where are the photoreceptors (rods and cones) located within the retina's layered histology, relative to the choroid and vitreous chamber?","choices":["Located exactly midway between the choroid and vitreous chamber","Located closest to the vitreous chamber, rather than near the choroid","Located closest to the choroid, rather than near the vitreous chamber","Located outside the retina entirely, within the choroid itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the histological organization of the retina, the photoreceptors (rods and cones) are located closest to the choroid, rather than near the vitreous chamber — light must pass through the other retinal layers before reaching them. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
